<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n27919" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mansŭētūdo</span></b> ,
			ĭnis, f.
		id., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>tameness</i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">elephanti,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Just. 15, 4, 19</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>A.</b> In gen., <i>mildness, gentleness, clemency</i> (class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">uti clementiā ac mansuetudine in
					aliquem,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Caes.
					B. G. 2, 14</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">imperii,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic. Verr. 2, 5, 44, § 114</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">morum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Off. 2, 9, 32</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">alterum genus orationis lenitatis et
					mansuetudinis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					de Or. 2, 49, 200</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">animorum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Off. 2, 4, 15</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hostes,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Tac. A.
					2, 72</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b> In
			partic., in the times of the emperors, a complimentary title used in
			addressing them: mansuetudo tua, <i>your clemency</i>
			or <i>your grace</i>, Eutr. praef. ad Valent.
		Imp.</div>
